          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What does guidance describe for an uncomplicated urinary tract infection in a non-pregnant adult?",
          "answer": "Guidance for managing an uncomplicated urinary tract infection (UTI) in a non-pregnant adult states that this type of infection refers to acute cystitis with a normal urinary tract and immune function. For diagnosis, if a person shows typical symptoms and has no vaginal irritation or discharge, it is appropriate to begin treatment without performing urine cultures. The likelihood of a UTI is high enough that empiric treatment is warranted. A urine dipstick test adds little value in this context. \n\nThis information is based on the \"Uncomplicated urinary tract infection: treatment, culture indications and recurrence\" guideline from the Infectious Diseases and Urology Society.\n\nI could not cover details about specific treatment options or management pathways for complicated UTIs.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
